      <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
    </div>
    <p>The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been presented at every ceremony since 1934, making it one of the most enduring categories in Oscar history. This dataset captures all <strong style="color:var(--gold-light)">93 winning entries</strong> across nine decades, encompassing <strong style="color:var(--gold-light)">79 unique individual composers</strong> and <strong style="color:var(--gold-light)">92 unique films</strong> (Oppenheimer appears in both the 2023 and 2024 data entries, credited to Ludwig G&ouml;ransson).</p>
    <p>Over its history, the category has undergone name changes and occasional splits (e.g., separate awards for dramatic and comedy/musical scores in certain periods), but the dataset consolidates to one winner per ceremony year, providing a clean longitudinal record of the Academy's evolving taste in film music.</p>
    <p>The winners span from the lush orchestral romanticism of Hollywood's Golden Age&mdash;represented by pioneers like Max Steiner and Alfred Newman&mdash;through the genre-spanning experimentation of the contemporary era, where electronic producers like Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross share the same lineage as traditional symphonists like John Williams.</p>

    <div class="note">
