      <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
    </div>

    <ol class="takeaway-list">
      <li><strong>Dominance is rare but real.</strong> Only 17 of 79 composers (21.5%) have won more than once, yet their repeat wins account for 28% of all ceremonies. The Oscar for Best Original Score is overwhelmingly a one-time honor, making the achievements of multi-winners all the more exceptional.</li>

      <li><strong>G&ouml;ransson is rewriting the record books in real time.</strong> With 4 wins by the 2026 ceremony&mdash;achieved faster than any predecessor&mdash;Ludwig G&ouml;ransson represents a new model of Oscar dominance in the Contemporary era. Whether he extends his lead will be the defining storyline of the late 2020s.</li>

      <li><strong>The 2010s were the great equalizer; the 2020s are consolidating.</strong> The 2010s saw perfect diversity (10 unique winners in 10 years), but the 2020s have already given 43% of their ceremonies to a single composer. This pattern of oscillation between diversity and concentration has repeated throughout the category's history.</li>

      <li><strong>The winning sonic palette has permanently expanded.</strong> The trajectory from pure orchestral (1930s&ndash;60s) through jazz and pop influence (1960s&ndash;70s) to electronic and global fusion (2000s&ndash;present) shows a one-way expansion. There is no indication the Academy will narrow its definition of what constitutes a winning score.</li>

      <li><strong>Internationalization accelerated after 2000.</strong> The Contemporary era has seen winners from India (A.R. Rahman), China (Tan Dun), Argentina (Gustavo Santaolalla), Iceland (Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir), Sweden (Ludwig G&ouml;ransson), and Germany (Volker Bertelmann), among others&mdash;a marked departure from the European-emigrant and American-born composers who dominated the first seven decades.</li>
    </ol>
